<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Anglo-Afghan War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Anglo-Afghan War was fought between the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. In 1839 a British army, marching through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, invaded, deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> and installed Shah Shuja. A revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> destroyed the retreating British garrison in January 1842. After a punitive expedition the British withdrew, and Dost Mohammad returned to the throne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war.jpg" alt="First Anglo-Afghan War" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_army_of_the_Indus_entering_Kandahar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war was one of the first major conflicts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>, the nineteenth-century competition between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. In Britain any Russian advance into Central Asia was assumed to be directed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, although no Russian tsar seriously planned an invasion. Lord Palmerston and John Hobhouse feared the instability of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the growing power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> kingdom. Scholars now also stress the fear that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> would ally to end Sikh rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. The British decided to replace Dost Mohammad with a more pliant ruler, the former king Shuja Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, some 21,000 British and Indian troops under John Keane, set out from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> with William Hay Macnaghten as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s chief representative, followed by 38,000 camp followers and 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>. The British occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> in August 1839 and restored Shuja. Most troops then went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, leaving 8,000 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, but Shuja's rule could be kept only by a British presence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> resented, and Macnaghten's decision to let soldiers bring their families seemed to show that the occupation would be permanent.</p><p>On 1 January 1842 an agreement allowed the British garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and its dependents to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, and the withdrawal began five days later. The column numbered about 16,500, of whom about 4,500 were soldiers and over 12,000 camp followers. Almost all of them died in the mountain passes. The garrisons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and Jalalabad held out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> was stormed. Akbar Khan was defeated near Jalalabad, and relief forces came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the spring.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Lord Ellenborough, who replaced Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> as governor-general, was under instructions to end the war. He ordered the forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and Jalalabad to leave after inflicting reprisals and securing the release of prisoners. Shuja Shah was assassinated and succeeded by his son Fateh Jang, who abdicated for his brother Shahpur Mirza. Their short reign ended with the return of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dost-mohammad-khan">Dost Mohammad Khan</a> from exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>The campaign cost over one million pounds in the year 1840 to 1841. In Britain Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a> criticised the war as rash, and the army chaplain G. R. Gleig wrote in 1843 that not one benefit was gained from it. In 1878 the British invaded again, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-anglo-afghan-war">Second Anglo-Afghan War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Anglo-Afghan War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The treaty Shuja Shah signed with the British East India Company on 7 June 1809, the first Afghan pact with a European power, pledged joint action against Franco-Persian aggression and foreshadowed the British restoration of Shuja in 1839 and the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>He was deposed by a British invasion in 1839 during the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b> but returned in 1842 and later reunited Kandahar and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b> the British occupied Kandahar from 1839 to 1842, and the sardars took refuge in Persia before returning.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kandahar">Principality of Kandahar</a></li><li><blockquote>The British forced treaties on the mirs, used Sindh during the <b>First Anglo-Afghan War</b> and then provoked war.</blockquote><a href="/state/talpur-dynasty">Talpur dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
